  "text_md": "# Burkina Faso\n\nbuilt classrooms and other education-related structures The Government of Burkina Faso has implemented or and ran awareness-raising programs on the importance of participated in social programs to address the worst forms of education.(95-97) Burkina Faso continued to take part in child labor in the production of cotton and in mining and the WFP Country Program (2011-2015) that promotes quarrying, improving access to education and social services. primary education and food security.(98) It also participated However, research found no evidence that it has carried out in a UNICEF-funded program to provide schooling to programs to address the worst forms of child labor found 5,000 child refugees in efforts to prevent the exploitation of in livestock raising, domestic service, forced begging, or refugee children.(99) street work***.***\n\n## Based on the reporting above, the following actions would advance the elimination of the worst\n\n## forms of child labor in Burkina Faso:\n\n|Area|Suggested Actions|Year(s) Action|\n|---|---|---|\n|||Recommended|\n|Laws and Regulations|Ensure children ages 12 to 15 are protected from hazardous conditions in domestic service and agriculture.|2010, 2011, 2012|\n|Coordination and Enforcement|Improve coordination across the relevant agencies working to reduce the worst forms of child labor. Increase labor inspector and enforcement officer resources to enable the enforcement of laws relating to the worst forms of child labor. Publicize detailed information on the numbers of prosecutions, convictions, and sentences for the worst forms of child labor. Establish and implement a systematic method for identifying victims of trafficking and other worst forms of child labor.|2011, 2012 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012 2009, 2010, 2011, 2012 2010, 2011, 2012|\n|Policies Social Programs|Streamline coordination of policies related to the worst forms of child labor and implement National Action Plans. Take measures to increase access to free, quality education that is safe for children by • Implementing programs to address violence in schools;|2009, 2010, 2011, 2012|\n\n2010, 2011, 2012\n\n- Expanding birth registration campaigns;\n- Building new schools and maintaining existing schools; and\n- Reducing or eliminating school-related fees. Institute programs to address the worst forms of child labor in livestock raising, 2009, 2010, 2011, domestic service, forced begging, and street work. 2012\n<u>REFERENCES</u> worst forms of child labor. For more information on sources used, the definition of working children and other indicators used in this report, please see the\n\n1. UNESCO Institute for Statistics. *Gross intake ratio to the last grade of primary.* “Children’s Work and Education Statistics: Sources and Definitions” section of *Total.*; accessed February 4, 2013; [http://www.uis.unesco.org/Pages/default](http://www.uis.unesco.org/Pages/default). this report. aspx?SPSLanguage=EN. Data provided is the gross intake ratio to the last grade 3. Diallo, Y. *Les Activites des Enfants en Afrique Subsaharienne: Les Enseignements* of primary school. This measure is a proxy measure for primary completion.\n*des Enquetes sur le Travail des Enfants en Afrique de l’Ouest*. Rome; July For more information, please see the “Children’s Work and Education Statistics: 2008. [http://www.ucw-project.org/attachment/STyacouba_SSA_act_](http://www.ucw-project.org/attachment/STyacouba_SSA_act_) Sources and Definitions” section of this report. enf20110224_151654%5B1%5D20110420_120304.pdf.\n\n2. UCW. *Analysis of Child Economic Activity and School Attendance Statistics from* 4. U.S. Embassy- Ouagadougou. *reporting, January 20, 2012*. *National Household or Child Labor Surveys*. February 5, 2013. Reliable statistical 5. U.S. Embassy-Ouagadougou. *reporting, February 1, 2013*. data on the worst forms of child labor are especially difficult to collect given the 6. De Lange, A. *Deprived Children and Education*. 